::CW_U02_L07_Q01::Passage: "Tessa held the cracked mug with both hands while her brother packed the last box. 'You can take the couch,' she said, 'but not this.'" What does the mug most likely do in the scene?{
~A. It proves the kitchen is expensive.#Incorrect. Nothing in the passage connects the mug to cost.
=B. It acts as a key object that carries emotional meaning.#Correct. The cracked mug becomes a focused detail that reveals attachment during a separation.
~C. It changes the point of view to second person.#Incorrect. A physical object does not determine second-person narration.
~D. It removes conflict from the scene.#Incorrect. The object actually sharpens the conflict over what matters.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q02::Passage: "I smiled at the camera until Mom looked away. Then I folded the acceptance letter into a square small enough to hide in my sleeve." Which choice best identifies the narrator effect?{
~A. First person shows every character's thoughts equally.#Incorrect. First person mainly gives access to the narrator's experience.
~B. First person makes the scene objective and emotionless.#Incorrect. The narration is personal and emotionally charged.
~C. First person removes the need for action.#Incorrect. The hidden letter action is central to meaning.
=D. First person reveals the narrator's private conflict between appearance and truth.#Correct. The reader sees both the public smile and the hidden action.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q03::Passage: Scene purpose: show that Marcus is tempted to betray a friend. Which detail best supports the purpose?{
=A. Marcus rests his hand on the folder with his friend's name while the principal waits.#Correct. The action, object, and waiting authority all focus the betrayal pressure.
~B. Marcus notices the office has gray carpet.#Incorrect. Neutral setting detail does not show temptation by itself.
~C. Marcus remembers lunch yesterday.#Incorrect. Background only helps if it connects directly to the betrayal.
~D. Marcus counts the ceiling tiles for fun.#Incorrect. This distracts unless connected to the central conflict.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q04::Passage: "The hallway stretched longer after the announcement. Every locker door clicked shut before Ana reached hers." What pacing effect is created?{
~A. The scene skips the important moment completely.#Incorrect. The passage lingers on the moment instead of skipping it.
~B. The pacing makes the scene comic and carefree.#Incorrect. The lengthened hallway and closing lockers create unease.
=C. The slow, stretched moment increases Ana's tension.#Correct. The sentence slows time and uses repeated sound to make the walk feel pressured.
~D. The pacing explains school rules.#Incorrect. The passage creates mood, not a rule explanation.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q05::Passage: A student wants to show that a character regrets lying. Which integrated choice is strongest?{
=A. Use close narration, a repeated image of the lie on a note, and a slower pause before confession.#Correct. Narration, object, and pacing all support the same emotional purpose.
~B. Add a random storm, change to future tense, and list furniture.#Incorrect. These choices do not clearly connect to regret.
~C. Use a distant narrator and summarize the entire year.#Incorrect. Summary may flatten the immediate regret.
~D. Describe the character's outfit in detail only.#Incorrect. Outfit details matter only if tied to the lie and regret.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q06::Passage: "When the bus doors opened, Devin stepped back instead of forward. The ticket in his palm had softened with sweat." Which conclusion is best supported?{
~A. Devin has already arrived at his destination.#Incorrect. The opening bus doors suggest the departure decision is happening now.
~B. Devin dislikes all buses.#Incorrect. The passage shows this moment's pressure, not a general dislike.
~C. Devin lost his ticket.#Incorrect. He is holding the ticket.
=D. Devin is uncertain about leaving.#Correct. His backward movement and sweaty ticket show hesitation.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q07::Passage: Scene goal: show quiet anger. Which sentence best fits?{
~A. Lina screamed for three pages about every problem.#Incorrect. Loud explanation may show anger, but not quiet anger.
=B. Lina smiled and stacked the plates so carefully that one cracked in her hand.#Correct. Controlled action plus the cracked plate reveals anger beneath restraint.
~C. Lina forgot the argument and went shopping.#Incorrect. This moves away from anger.
~D. Lina described the tablecloth pattern neutrally.#Incorrect. Neutral description does not reveal the emotion.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q08::Passage: "He told the story twice, and both times the locked gate became taller." What narrative technique is most important?{
~A. Dialogue formatting.#Incorrect. The passage is about how the story changes.
~B. Exact measurement.#Incorrect. The changing height is not likely literal measurement.
=C. Unreliable or subjective narration.#Correct. The repeated story changes, showing memory or perception may be shaping the account.
~D. Alphabetical order.#Incorrect. No list or alphabetical structure is involved.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q09::Passage: "The violin case sat under Sari's bed for six months. On the morning of the audition, she pulled it out and dust marked her sleeves." Which revision best heightens the turning point?{
~A. Add a paragraph about how dust is made.#Incorrect. That distracts from the decision moment.
~B. Remove the audition so nothing changes.#Incorrect. The audition gives the scene a meaningful pressure point.
~C. List every object under the bed.#Incorrect. Extra objects may dilute focus.
=D. Add one sentence showing Sari opening the case after hesitating.#Correct. The hesitation and opening would make the change immediate and visible.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q10::Passage: A scene uses second-person narration: "You keep your eyes on the exit sign, pretending you are only counting the letters." What is a likely effect?{
=A. It can place the reader inside the character's anxious behavior.#Correct. Second person can create immediacy by making the action feel directed at "you."
~B. It automatically makes the scene factual nonfiction.#Incorrect. Point of view does not determine genre by itself.
~C. It prevents the writer from using setting.#Incorrect. The exit sign is setting detail.
~D. It removes the character's inner life.#Incorrect. The line suggests anxiety through behavior.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q11::Passage: "Mira placed the scholarship letter beside the unpaid bill, then turned both facedown." What relationship between elements is strongest?{
~A. The two papers are unrelated props.#Incorrect. Their placement together invites comparison.
~B. The unpaid bill is comic relief.#Incorrect. It creates pressure, not comedy.
=C. The objects reveal a conflict between opportunity and financial stress.#Correct. The letter and bill together show what the decision may cost or require.
~D. The scene is mainly about paper texture.#Incorrect. Texture is not the focus.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q12::Passage: Scene purpose: show a friendship beginning to repair. Which pacing choice fits best?{
~A. Rush past the apology in one summary sentence.#Incorrect. The repair moment needs enough space for readers to feel the change.
=B. Slow the moment when one character returns a borrowed jacket and waits for a response.#Correct. Slowing the exchange lets action, object, and reaction carry the repair.
~C. Insert five pages about unrelated sports scores.#Incorrect. Unrelated information weakens focus.
~D. End before the friends interact.#Incorrect. The repair cannot be shown clearly without interaction.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q13::Passage: "Dad's old watch stopped at 4:12, the minute our train should have arrived." How does the watch function?{
=A. It connects time, memory, and the scene's emotional pressure.#Correct. The stopped watch turns time into a meaningful detail.
~B. It only tells readers the brand of the watch.#Incorrect. No brand is named.
~C. It proves the narrator is lying.#Incorrect. The passage alone does not establish deception.
~D. It removes the need for setting.#Incorrect. The train context is part of the setting.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q14::Passage: "By the time the judge called my name, the poem in my pocket felt heavier than the trophy on the table." What is being integrated?{
~A. Cooking and weather.#Incorrect. Neither appears.
~B. A random object and unrelated prize.#Incorrect. The poem and trophy are related through the contest pressure.
~C. A flashback only.#Incorrect. The passage happens in the present moment.
=D. Character desire, object symbolism, and public stakes.#Correct. The poem, trophy, and judge's call combine to reveal what matters.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q15::Passage: A writer wants the reader to distrust a narrator. Which choice works best?{
~A. The narrator uses complete sentences.#Incorrect. Correct grammar does not create distrust.
~B. The narrator mentions a blue wall once.#Incorrect. A color detail alone does not make narration unreliable.
=C. The narrator describes the same event differently each time and avoids one question.#Correct. Contradiction and avoidance give readers evidence to question the narrator.
~D. The narrator stands outside.#Incorrect. Location alone does not affect trust.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q16::Passage: "The candle flame bent toward the open window as Sam read the unsigned note." Which integrated interpretation is strongest?{
~A. The window proves the scene is cheerful.#Incorrect. The unsigned note and bending flame create uncertainty.
=B. The setting detail mirrors the unstable mood of the note.#Correct. The flame's movement works with the mysterious note to create unease.
~C. The candle is unrelated to mood.#Incorrect. In this context, the flame contributes to mood.
~D. The note explains how candles work.#Incorrect. The note creates conflict or mystery.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q17::Passage: Scene goal: show that a character chooses courage. Which ending best completes the scene?{
~A. The character leaves before the challenge appears.#Incorrect. Courage requires facing pressure.
~B. The character remembers being tired and goes home.#Incorrect. This avoids the central challenge.
~C. The character describes the wallpaper for two paragraphs.#Incorrect. Description alone does not complete the courage arc.
=D. The character opens the door after hearing the crowd go silent.#Correct. The action occurs at a tense moment and shows courage through choice.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q18::Passage: "I kept saying the river was shallow, but I would not step off the bridge." What does the contrast reveal?{
=A. The narrator's words and actions do not match.#Correct. Saying the river is shallow while refusing to step forward reveals fear or self-deception.
~B. The bridge has disappeared.#Incorrect. The narrator is still on the bridge.
~C. The river is not part of the setting.#Incorrect. The river is central to the moment.
~D. The scene has no conflict.#Incorrect. The mismatch creates inner conflict.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q19::Passage: A draft scene has a clear conflict but begins with two pages of unrelated family history. What revision best improves pacing?{
~A. Keep all history before the conflict so readers wait longer.#Incorrect. Unrelated delay can weaken tension.
=B. Move only the necessary background into brief details near the conflict.#Correct. This keeps the scene focused while giving needed context.
~C. Remove the conflict and keep only history.#Incorrect. The scene needs the conflict.
~D. Add more unrelated history.#Incorrect. More unrelated material increases the problem.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q20::Passage: "The map was wrong, but Jules folded it carefully anyway." What does this detail suggest?{
~A. Jules throws the map away.#Incorrect. The passage says Jules folds it.
~B. The scene is only about geography facts.#Incorrect. The action reveals character.
=C. Jules respects or needs the map even after it fails.#Correct. Folding the wrong map carefully shows attachment, habit, or hope.
~D. The map has no role.#Incorrect. The map is the key object.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q21::Passage: Which planning sequence best creates an integrated narrative scene?{
=A. Pick a purpose, choose point of view for that purpose, select key details, and control pacing around the turn.#Correct. This sequence makes each craft choice serve the same scene goal.
~B. Choose random details first and decide meaning later.#Incorrect. Random details often make the scene unfocused.
~C. Write only dialogue with no context.#Incorrect. Dialogue usually needs action, setting, and purpose.
~D. Avoid revision after drafting.#Incorrect. Integration often improves during revision.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q22::Passage: "The classroom clock clicked through the silence after Omar said, 'I was the one who erased it.'" What is the function of the clock?{
~A. It gives a math lesson.#Incorrect. The clock is not used for calculation.
~B. It changes the genre to fantasy.#Incorrect. Nothing supernatural appears.
=C. It slows the moment after a confession.#Correct. The ticking sound stretches the silence and increases consequence.
~D. It proves Omar is innocent.#Incorrect. Omar has confessed.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q23::Passage: A student says, "My scene has a necklace, a storm, and a secret, so it is integrated." What is the best response?{
~A. Integration means every scene must include three objects.#Incorrect. Integration is not a required object count.
~B. Integration means the author writes quickly.#Incorrect. Speed is not the standard.
~C. Integration means removing all setting.#Incorrect. Setting can be essential.
=D. Integration means the pieces work together to develop character, conflict, and meaning.#Correct. The necklace, storm, and secret matter only if they support a unified purpose.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q24::Passage: "After the argument, June washed the same clean glass three times." Which interpretation best fits?{
~A. June is practicing for a dishwashing contest.#Incorrect. The context points to emotional avoidance, not competition.
=B. June is using repeated action to manage feelings she has not said aloud.#Correct. The repeated washing shows inner conflict through behavior.
~C. June has no reaction to the argument.#Incorrect. Repeating an unnecessary action suggests reaction.
~D. June wants to describe soap.#Incorrect. Soap is not the focus.
}
::CW_U02_L07_Q25::Passage: Scene purpose: show a character accepting responsibility. Which detail best supports the purpose?{
=A. The character signs their name under the apology instead of leaving it anonymous.#Correct. The action visibly shows ownership of the mistake.
~B. The character hides the apology in a drawer forever.#Incorrect. Hiding it avoids responsibility.
~C. The character blames the weather.#Incorrect. Blame shifts responsibility away.
~D. The character changes the topic to lunch.#Incorrect. Avoiding the issue weakens the purpose.
}
